  "response": "# Verdict: REJECT\n\n**Overall Assessment**: \nThe `BuggyExpandable` component is an inaccessible custom disclosure widget constructed entirely from unadorned `<div>` elements. It lacks native button semantics, keyboard operability, ARIA state communication, and programmatic association between the trigger and content panel, rendering it completely inoperable for keyboard-only and screen reader users.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\n- *Predicted*: Disclosure trigger implemented as a `<div>` without keyboard focus/events (`tabindex`, `onKeyDown`, or native `<button>`), missing `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls`, unhidden visual symbols announced verbatim to screen readers, missing heading structure context.\n- *Actual*: Confirmed all predictions. The component exhibits every classic anti-pattern of an unsemantic `<div>`-based accordion/disclosure widget.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### Critical Findings (Blocks Access)\n\n1. **Trigger is a non-interactive `<div>` lacking keyboard operability and accessible role**\n   - **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:11-17`\n     ```jsx\n     <div\n       className=\"expandable-trigger\"\n       onClick={toggle}\n     >\n     ```\n   - **Impacted User Group**: Keyboard-only users, switch device users, screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A), WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A), and WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure (Show/Hide) Pattern.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: A `<div>` has no implicit keyboard focus (`tabindex`), cannot be reached via <kbd>Tab</kbd>, and does not fire keydown handlers for <kbd>Enter</kbd> or <kbd>Space</kbd>. Assistive technology announces it as generic text rather than an actionable control.\n   - **Fix**: Replace the `<div>` with a semantic native HTML `<button type=\"button\">`. Native buttons provide implicit keyboard focus, Enter/Space activation, and correct role semantics out of the box (\"Native HTML first\").\n\n2. **Missing programmatic expansion state (`aria-expanded`)**\n   - **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:11-17`\n   - **Impacted User Group**: Screen reader users, blind / low-vision users.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A), WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Pattern.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: Assistive tech users receive zero indication of whether the section is currently expanded or collapsed. Sighted users see the content appear/disappear, but screen reader users are left unaware that dynamic content was toggled.\n   - **Fix**: Add `aria-expanded={isExpanded}` to the trigger element.\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)\n\n1. **Missing programmatic relationship between trigger and controlled content (`aria-controls` & `id`)**\n   - **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:19-23`\n     ```jsx\n     {isExpanded && (\n       <div className=\"expandable-content\">\n         {content}\n       </div>\n     )}\n     ```\n   - **Impacted User Group**: Screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships (Level A), WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Pattern.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: Screen readers cannot announce or navigate directly to the region controlled by the disclosure button without an explicit programmatic association.\n   - **Fix**: Assign a unique `id` (e.g., via React's `useId()`) to `.expandable-content` and reference it from the trigger using `aria-controls={contentId}`.\n\n2. **Visual state symbols (`+` / `\u2212`) exposed unmasked to screen readers**\n   - **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:16`\n     ```jsx\n     {isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}\n     ```\n   - **Impacted User Group**: Screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A), WCAG 1.1.1 Non-text Content (Level A).\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: Screen readers will announce the literal glyph (\"plus\", \"minus\", or \"hyphen-minus\") as part of the button's accessible name (e.g., *\"Account Details plus, button\"*), creating noisy, confusing, and redundant speech when state is already communicated via `aria-expanded`.\n   - **Fix**: Wrap the text symbol in `<span aria-hidden=\"true\">{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}</span>`.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings (Friction but Workaround Exists)\n\n- **Missing heading container**: In accordion and multi-section layouts, disclosure buttons should typically be wrapped in an appropriate semantic heading level (`<h2>`, `<h3>`, etc.) so screen reader users can navigate directly between sections using heading navigation keys (<kbd>H</kbd> / <kbd>1-6</kbd>).\n\n---\n\n### Enhancements (Best Practice)\n\n- **Consider native `<details>` and `<summary>`**: If custom accordion behaviors (like multi-panel orchestration or complex animations) are not strictly required, using `<details>` and `<summary>` provides fully accessible disclosure functionality with zero JavaScript and native browser accessibility support.\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing\n\n- **Keyboard handlers**: No `onKeyDown` support for <kbd>Space</kbd> or <kbd>Enter</kbd> (automatically resolved by adopting `<button>`).\n- **Focus styling / Visible focus indicator**: The `<div>` lacks standard browser focus outlines; custom styling must ensure compliance with WCAG 2.4.7 (Focus Visible) and 2.4.13 (Focus Appearance) once made focusable.\n- **Unique ID generator**: Missing unique ID linkage (`useId()`) for pairing `aria-controls` with the disclosure panel.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Trigger has generic `div` role; `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls` missing; literal `+`/`\u2212` symbols read aloud. |\n| Keyboard-only | **HIGH** | Trigger element is completely skipped during Tab navigation; cannot be activated via keyboard. |\n| Low vision | **MEDIUM** | Requires verification of visible focus indicator ring (\u22653:1 contrast) and touch target size (\u226524\u00d724px, ideally 44\u00d744px). |\n| Cognitive | **LOW** | Layout and toggle concept are simple, but clear button affordance and consistent labeling are required. |\n| Vestibular & motion | **LOW** | No motion or transition triggers currently present. |\n| Auditory access | **LOW** | No audio or video content involved. |\n| Environmental contrast | **MEDIUM** | Dependent on CSS styling for `.expandable-trigger` and `:focus-visible`. |\n\n- **Screen Reader User**: Hears static text with an arbitrary \"plus\" or \"minus\" symbol. Cannot tell this element is actionable or that content can be revealed.\n- **Keyboard-only User**: Completely locked out. The trigger cannot receive focus and cannot be activated via keyboard.\n- **Low-Vision User**: Needs an explicit, high-contrast focus indicator once the element becomes focusable.\n- **Cognitive Accessibility**: Lacks standard button visual affordance, relying purely on hover/cursor styling if any exists in CSS.\n\n---\n\n## Recommended Remediation\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { useState, useId } from 'react';\n\nconst AccessibleExpandable = ({ title, content, headingLevel = 3 }) => {\n  const [isExpanded, setIsExpanded] = useState(false);\n  const contentId = useId();\n  const HeadingTag = `h${headingLevel}`;\n\n  const toggle = () => {\n    setIsExpanded((prev) => !prev);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div className=\"expandable-section\">\n      <HeadingTag className=\"expandable-heading\">\n        <button\n          type=\"button\"\n          className=\"expandable-trigger\"\n          onClick={toggle}\n          aria-expanded={isExpanded}\n          aria-controls={contentId}\n        >\n          <span className=\"expandable-title\">{title}</span>\n          <span className=\"expandable-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n            {isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}\n          </span>\n        </button>\n      </HeadingTag>\n\n      <div\n        id={contentId}\n        className=\"expandable-content\"\n        hidden={!isExpanded}\n      >\n        {content}\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  );\n};\n\nexport default AccessibleExpandable;\n```\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\n**REJECT**. The component violates foundational Level A accessibility criteria (WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard and WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value). By using an unadorned `<div>` with only an `onClick` listener, it creates a complete barrier for keyboard users and fails to communicate any role or state to assistive technologies. It requires structural refactoring to use a native `<button>` element with `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls` before it can be accepted.\n",
